# Chaos: a cap on the memory of thing textures (src/client/thingtype.h/.cpp, thingtypemanager.h/.cpp, luafunctions_client.cpp; after 0001-0124).
# The base frees a thing texture only after 60 s without use (ThingTypeManager::check, 100 types per category per second),
# so a long walk over many different outfits, items and effects (HD scale 3-4 makes every texture 9-16 times larger) kept
# all of them until the minute passed. The sprite pixel cache (SpriteManager) was already capped; the textures were not.
# Now ThingType counts the bytes of the textures it builds (width x height x 4, per animation phase) and ThingTypeManager::check
# (once a second) calls enforceTextureCap(): over the cap, the loaded types are unloaded oldest use first until the total is
# at 90% of the cap. A type used in the last 5 s is never unloaded, so what the next frames draw is not thrown away (no thrash).
# The cap is the setting textureCacheMB (g_settings.set('textureCacheMB', n); default 256, 0 = no cap, below 64 raised to 64),
# read at each check, so it takes effect without a restart. Lua: g_things.getTextureCacheBytes / getTextureCacheLimitBytes /
# getTextureCacheEvictions. Unload and rebuild are the base's own path (ThingType::unload, getTexture), nothing else changes.
# Not run in a full build or measured in play; touched files pass g++ -fsyntax-only with the patch chain applied.
